# sveltekit-fsd

This skill is repository-wide. It applies to any package in this repository
that has a `sveltekit-fsd.config.json`; the package's own `AGENTS.md` and
`docs/fsd.md` hold the exact paths and any product-specific rules. This file
only explains how to drive the generator and where the boundaries are.

## Find the target package first

1. Locate the nearest `sveltekit-fsd.config.json` from the directory the user
   means. If the repository has several package configs, do not guess: name the
   candidates and ask which package the change belongs to.
2. Read that config. `features.errorHandling` and `features.auth` decide which
   flags are legal, `routesDir` says where routes live, `locale` says which
   language generated copy uses, and the import spelling is recorded by
   `init` (`@` on SvelteKit 2, `#` on SvelteKit 3).
3. Read the target package's `AGENTS.md` and `docs/fsd.md`. If they disagree
   with this skill, the package docs are the source of truth for that package.
4. Run every command from the target package's root, not from the repository
   root.

## Commands

Pass every required value explicitly: an omitted value opens a prompt, and in a
non-interactive shell that exits 1 without writing anything.

~~~bash
sveltekit-fsd wizard
sveltekit-fsd init [--locale th|en] [--no-install] [--no-hooks]
sveltekit-fsd generate page <name...> [--route <path>] [--no-route] [--auth] [--api] [--errors] [-r <root>] [--defaults]
sveltekit-fsd generate slice <features|entities|widgets> <name...> [-s <segments...>] [-r <root>] [--errors] [--defaults]
sveltekit-fsd generate layout <name> [--route <path>] [--no-route] [--guard] [--defaults]
sveltekit-fsd add error-handling [-y] [--no-install]
sveltekit-fsd add auth [-y] [--no-install]
sveltekit-fsd add prettier [-y] [--no-install]
sveltekit-fsd config show
~~~

Feature gates are deliberate:

- `--auth` and `--guard` require `features.auth`; run `add auth` first.
- `--errors`, `--api`, and the `api` segment require `features.errorHandling`;
  run `add error-handling` first.
- `add auth` installs error handling itself when it is missing.
- `add prettier` formats the whole package once on purpose; commit that pass
  separately.

## Extend, do not rewrite

Re-run the same command to add to an existing slice. Do not hand-write files the
CLI owns, and do not delete a slice to regenerate it.

Only missing files are written; new exports are appended; existing files are
left alone. If there is nothing to add, the command says so and writes nothing
— that is the expected answer, not a failure to work around.

## Boundaries that fail silently

- Imports point downwards only: `app -> pages -> widgets -> features ->
  entities -> shared`.
- A slice is entered through its `index.ts`; importing a slice's internal file
  is a boundary violation.
- A module using Svelte runes is named `*.svelte.ts` and imported without the
  `.ts`.
- `createQuery`, `createMutation`, and `useQueryClient` are called during
  component initialisation; their options are a function.
- A route file renders a page and nothing else. The title belongs to the page
  component's own `<svelte:head>`.
- Page-specific requests live in the page slice's `api/`; reusable domain
  requests belong to `entities/<name>/api/`; reusable user actions to
  `features/<name>/api/`; generic CRUD/client primitives to `shared/api/`.
- Shared UI is in `shared/ui`; do not grow SvelteKit's own `lib/` directory.
- The package's `AGENTS.md` and `docs/fsd.md` are authoritative when this
  summary is not enough.

## Auth and error handling

The generated auth/error code is a starting adapter, not a mandate. Read the
package's `shared/api/` and `shared/auth/` before changing it. If the backend
does not match the default go-scaffold response shape, keep the package's
public client contract and replace the adapter; do not copy a second client.

A 401 from `/auth/*` means wrong credentials or an expired challenge; do not
refresh it. A 401 that survives one refresh means the session is over, regardless
of which request found it.

## Verification

Run the target package's own scripts, not a generic command from the repository
root:

~~~bash
<package manager> run lint
<package manager> run check
<package manager> run build
~~~

Run the package's test command too. Report the exact output and say which
generated TODOs are still TODOs. A passing build is not a completed business
rule.
